{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LHbi5DxZtdqA6zRmRSQdV8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Patch task (step 2 of 3): write a code override, not weights\n2\t\n3\tLaya routes customer messages to one of 5 departments: cards, disputes, transfers_topups, fees_fx, account_security.\n4\t`router.py` holds the current routing patch: `route(text, laya_dept) -> dept`.\n5\tIt receives the message and Laya's current answer and returns the final answer.\n6\t\n7\t## Department descriptions given to the model\n8\t- cards: getting, activating, delivering, replacing or using a payment card; PIN, contactless, virtual and disposable cards, Apple Pay or Google Pay, lost or stolen cards\n9\t- disputes: a payment, withdrawal or debit the customer does not recognise, was declined, is pending, was charged twice or reverted; refunds\n10\t- transfers_topups: sending or receiving money, bank transfers, topping up the account, balance not updated after a transfer or deposit\n11\t- fees_fx: fees and charges, exchange rates, currency exchange, supported currencies\n12\t- account_security: identity verification, passcode, personal details, closing the account, lost phone, age or country eligibility\n13\t\n14\t## Corrections already applied in earlier steps (must stay true)\n15\t1. Complaints that a card payment or a cash withdrawal used a bad or wrong exchange rate belong to fees_fx, not disputes, even when the customer says the amount looks wrong.\n16\t\n17\t## New correction for this step\n18\tAnything about a card PIN, such as changing the PIN or a PIN that is blocked, belongs to cards, not account_security.\n19\t\n20\t`evidence.csv` has 32 labelled messages for this correction (text, Laya's\n21\tcurrent answer, correct department).\n22\t\n23\tEdit `router.py` so the final answers follow the new correction for all such\n24\tmessages, keep earlier corrections, and otherwise keep Laya's answer. Plain Python\n25\tonly (re, string ops); no ML libraries, no training. A hidden test set scores it.\n26\tTo test your patch, use this Python: `<lab>/.venv/bin/python`.\n27\tWrite `NOTES.md` (under 200 words).\n28\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6f0c811-fa84-486e-a3d5-34d547807295","uuid":"61e0efc4-02e0-4ed3-baa1-369d4a7f860b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:24:05.642Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"$WSROOT/laya/s0/fable-high-code-r2/step2/TASK.md","content":"# Patch task (step 2 of 3): write a code override, not weights\n\nLaya routes customer messages to one of 5 departments: cards, disputes, transfers_topups, fees_fx, account_security.\n`router.py` holds the current routing patch: `route(text, laya_dept) -> dept`.\nIt receives the message and Laya's current answer and returns the final answer.\n\n## Department descriptions given to the model\n- cards: getting, activating, delivering, replacing or using a payment card; PIN, contactless, virtual and disposable cards, Apple Pay or Google Pay, lost or stolen cards\n- disputes: a payment, withdrawal or debit the customer does not recognise, was declined, is pending, was charged twice or reverted; refunds\n- transfers_topups: sending or receiving money, bank transfers, topping up the account, balance not updated after a transfer or deposit\n- fees_fx: fees and charges, exchange rates, currency exchange, supported currencies\n- account_security: identity verification, passcode, personal details, closing the account, lost phone, age or country eligibility\n\n## Corrections already applied in earlier steps (must stay true)\n1. Complaints that a card payment or a cash withdrawal used a bad or wrong exchange rate belong to fees_fx, not disputes, even when the customer says the amount looks wrong.\n\n## New correction for this step\nAnything about a card PIN, such as changing the PIN or a PIN that is blocked, belongs to cards, not account_security.\n\n`evidence.csv` has 32 labelled messages for this correction (text, Laya's\ncurrent answer, correct department).\n\nEdit `router.py` so the final answers follow the new correction for all such\nmessages, keep earlier corrections, and otherwise keep Laya's answer. Plain Python\nonly (re, string ops); no ML libraries, no training. A hidden test set scores it.\nTo test your patch, use this Python: `<lab>/.venv/bin/python`.\nWrite `NOTES.md` (under 200 words).\n","numLines":28,"startLine":1,"totalLines":28}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LHbi5DxZtdqA6zRmRSQdV8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
